    "method": "Inversion of ascending and descending Sentinel-1 interferometric line-of-sight displacements using nonlinear and linear elastic dislocation methods; published spatial slip distribution digitized from Figure 8b.",
    "green_function_model": "Elastic half-space rectangular dislocations using Okada formalism.",
    "slip_type": "variable",
    "smoothing_constraint": false,
    "notes": "Figure 8b native rectangular display comprises 15 along-strike columns × 10 down-dip rows, over 18 × 12 km, giving 1.2 × 1.2 km image cells. Slip is reconstructed per cell from the printed 0–1.0 m colorbar, excluding gridlines and slip-direction arrows. This is IMAGE DIGITIZATION, not the authors numerical inversion coefficients. The source publication reports maximum slip 1.03 m at approximately 3.5–5 km depth, fault strike 216 deg, dip 53 deg, rake -95 deg, and moment 1.14e18 N m. Mean slip below is an unweighted reconstructed entire-plane mean, not a published mean. The figure does not explicitly state the actual absolute top-depth of the fault plane: top_depth_km=0 and patch depth_km values use an illustrative surface-anchored coordinate convention, NOT independently confirmed true depths. The model patch orientations are for browser display; geographic patch centroids are not published and are null. The hypocenter latitude, longitude and depth are the common-event reference values from existing Famiglietti et al. model (35.1512 N,25.2736 E,10 km), not coordinates reported by Triantafyllou et al. It should not be represented as a paper-specific hypocenter estimate. Low-green slips were treated as zero-compatible within color precision. The black arrows encode rake directions and were deliberately not transcribed as subfault-specific rake angles: only the published average rake -95 deg is used. The publication does not explicitly document a smoothing constraint in this section. smoothing_constraint=false is a conservative schema-compatible stand-in for an unconfirmed state, not evidence that smoothing was absent."
  },
